  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1235 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nHot Tuesday:\n\nThe hot and dry weather looks to continue through much of\nTuesday. In fact, NBM percentile data suggest Tuesday may be the\nhottest day of the forecast period as a ridge axis builds over\nthe Missouri Ozarks. If currently forecasted temperatures\nverify, daily record max temperatures would be broken or tied \nat all four climate sites.\n\nRain and Thunderstorm Chances Mid-week:\n\nA more active pattern returns as we proceed into late Tuesday \nand through mid- week thanks to a pair of tropical systems in \nthe Pacific and the aforementioned shortwave trough currently\nover the Pacific Northwest. Global ensembles continue to depict \ngood agreement in ingesting the two tropical systems into the \nsouthwest flow across the western CONUS, resulting in a stream \nof rich moisture content ahead of the upper-level shortwave. \nCluster analysis depicts somewhat better agreement among\nensembles regarding the evolution of the upper-level pattern\ncompared to yesterday, but meaningful differences still persist. \nThe majority of ensembles depict convection developing along a \ncold front as it sags through Missouri late Tuesday before \neventually stalling out and lifting back to the northeast as a \nwarm front when the parent upper- level trough pivots through \nthe Midwest sometime late Thursday into Friday. This solution \nwould result in a period of rain chances that last through \nThursday and possibly into Friday. The alternative is much more\nuncertain, with some ensembles--mostly ECMWF members--wanting \nto close off a low over the Intermountain West and effectively \nstall out the wave's progression, never phasing it with the \nnorthern stream energy. Should this happen, then the positioning\nof this wave/low will be of utmost importance, as it could \neither mean a sustained period of rain chances across the \nMissouri Ozarks, or some locations missing out entirely. A \nglance at NAEFS Ensemble Situational Awareness Tables reveals a \nstrong signal for abundant moisture content in forecast PWATs \nand specific humidity across southwest Missouri and southeast \nKansas where convection does form. Indeed, WPC's Day 3-7 Hazards\nOutlook map encompasses the entire forecast area in a risk for \nheavy rain. Current NBM rain chances increase Tuesday night \n(30-60%), max out on Wednesday (70-90%), and decrease Thursday \nnight (25-50%).\n\nAs we have seen recently, however, many factors will have to\nalign for this risk of heavy rain to be realized, and the\nforecast can and will change over the next 72 hours, so stay\ntuned as we get a better handle on these important\nmeteorological details. \n\nCooler temperatures will move in behind this system, with near\nto perhaps even below average temperatures by the weekend.",
